  "image_name": "35d942ec201e652367e9e90874541ac8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares charge-transfer pathways at a metal-oxide/perovskite interface under four conditions: no passivation, metal-oxide passivation, perovskite passivation, and double-side passivation. In each case, the top sketches show the interface structure, while the bottom diagrams show the conduction band (CB), valence band (VB), and interfacial in-gap states that can trap carriers.\n\nWithout passivation, many competing arrows connect the two materials and the defect states, indicating multiple recombination and trapping routes for electrons and holes across the junction. Passivating only the metal-oxide side reduces some defect-assisted pathways on that side, but several loss channels remain. Passivating only the perovskite side similarly suppresses traps associated with the perovskite, again leaving other interfacial recombination routes active.\n\nThe double-side passivation case is visually much simpler: the defect-state boxes disappear and only a few desired transfer arrows remain, mainly showing efficient carrier extraction with far fewer recombination pathways. Overall, the image conveys that both sides of the interface contribute to interfacial defects, and that simultaneous passivation of the metal oxide and perovskite is most effective at cleaning up the band alignment and minimizing nonradiative carrier loss.",
